  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new procedure removes a control you used for years because the control is now built into the system. The old method remains personally efficient because you know it well. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Verify the system control works as intended, then stop duplicating the old manual control$qb$, $qb$Check the new control during transition and follow the updated procedure$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual control permanently$qb$, $qb$Use the old control only on important cases$qb$, $qb$Ignore the updated procedure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new workflow changes who approves your work. The team must maintain delivery while adapting to the change. What should you do before the next submission?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the new approver and follow the updated handoff process$qb$, $qb$Check the updated workflow and ask if anything is unclear$qb$, $qb$Send it to both old and new approvers$qb$, $qb$Keep sending it to the old approver until told directly$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever approver replies faster$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for an urgent update while you are waiting for one fact from another team. Both requests have valid consequences for the business. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Share what is confirmed, clearly state what is pending, and give the expected update time$qb$, $qb$Give the confirmed information and mention the missing fact$qb$, $qb$Wait until the missing fact arrives before replying$qb$, $qb$Guess the missing fact so the update is complete$qb$, $qb$Say the work is delayed without explaining why$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared task has several small parts, but nobody knows who will combine them into the final version. The work crosses several ownership boundaries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree one person to assemble the final version and define when each part must reach them$qb$, $qb$Choose a final owner and tell everyone where to send their part$qb$, $qb$Let everyone upload their part and combine them at the end$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to assemble the final version$qb$, $qb$Wait until all parts are finished before deciding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manual check catches almost no issues but takes significant time. The improvement could become the normal method if it proves reliable. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Review the risk the check is meant to control and test a more efficient way to manage it$qb$, $qb$Collect evidence on what the check has caught before suggesting a change$qb$, $qb$Stop the check because it rarely finds problems$qb$, $qb$Automate the check immediately without reviewing its purpose$qb$, $qb$Keep it unchanged because it is part of the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are annoyed by repeated questions from a colleague during a busy period. You must continue the discussion constructively after the challenge. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Notice your frustration, answer respectfully, and agree a better way to group future questions$qb$, $qb$Answer the current question and suggest a set time for later questions$qb$, $qb$Give very short answers so the interruptions end faster$qb$, $qb$Tell them they ask too many questions$qb$, $qb$Stop responding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
