  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You feel defensive when a colleague questions your work in a meeting. You must make another consequential judgement immediately afterward. What is the best immediate response?$qb$, $qb$Pause, listen to the concern, and answer the point rather than the tone$qb$, $qb$Ask for a moment to understand the concern before replying$qb$, $qb$Explain why your work is correct before they continue$qb$, $qb$Stay silent for the rest of the meeting$qb$, $qb$Respond sharply so the criticism does not continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI summary leaves out a number that appears important in the source document. The output may influence a material customer or management decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the source and correct the summary before using it$qb$, $qb$Compare the summary with the relevant source section$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to produce the summary again$qb$, $qb$Use the summary but mention that AI created it$qb$, $qb$Assume the omitted number was not important$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new tool removes a manual step you have used for years. The old method remains personally effective but creates inconsistency across the wider operation. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Learn the new flow and stop the old step once the new method is verified$qb$, $qb$Use the new tool while checking results against the old method at first$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual step as a permanent backup$qb$, $qb$Use the old method for important cases$qb$, $qb$Avoid the new tool until everyone else is comfortable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team tool changes and one feature you used often is removed. The team must preserve delivery while adapting to the change. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Learn the replacement method and adjust your routine$qb$, $qb$Check the guidance and ask about the replacement feature$qb$, $qb$Keep searching for a way to use the old feature$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate to handle tasks needing that feature$qb$, $qb$Avoid those tasks until the feature returns$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager's verbal instruction differs from the written task note. You own the outcome, but multiple senior expectations need to be reconciled. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm which instruction should guide the work before proceeding$qb$, $qb$Point out the difference and ask for clarification$qb$, $qb$Follow the verbal instruction because it is newer$qb$, $qb$Follow the written note because it is documented$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever instruction is easier$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project handoff is delayed because everyone thought someone else would send the file. Several workstreams are individually progressing while shared dependencies are becoming risky. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Agree one named owner and a clear handoff time for the next cycle$qb$, $qb$Add the handoff to the team checklist$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to remember the file next time$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to send the file in future$qb$, $qb$Let the receiving team chase the file$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A simple trial of a new method saves time with no quality loss. The improvement may become standard practice, so scale and exception handling matter. What should you do next?$qb$, $qb$Document the result and propose a controlled wider trial$qb$, $qb$Repeat the method on a few similar tasks$qb$, $qb$Replace the old method immediately for all work$qb$, $qb$Keep using it only for your own tasks$qb$, $qb$Return to the old method because it is approved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague criticizes your idea and you feel an urge to interrupt. You must make another consequential judgement soon after the interaction. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Let them finish, notice your reaction, and answer the substance of the criticism$qb$, $qb$Listen fully and ask one question before replying$qb$, $qb$Interrupt briefly to correct a factual point$qb$, $qb$Stay silent even when it is your turn to respond$qb$, $qb$Interrupt so your idea is not misunderstood$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool gives two different answers to the same factual question. The output may materially affect a customer, financial, or operational decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the fact using a reliable independent source before using either answer$qb$, $qb$Compare the two answers and identify where they differ$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI a third time and use the majority answer$qb$, $qb$Choose the answer that sounds more detailed$qb$, $qb$Use the first answer because it came first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new approved system now performs a calculation you used to do manually. The old approach remains locally effective but weakens consistency across the broader operation. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Verify the new calculation during transition, then stop the manual duplicate once confidence is established$qb$, $qb$Compare the new result with your manual calculation for a short period$qb$, $qb$Keep doing both calculations permanently$qb$, $qb$Trust only the manual calculation for important work$qb$, $qb$Avoid the new system$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process changes while you are halfway through a task. The team must sustain normal performance while the change is embedded. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check which version applies to the current task and adjust accordingly$qb$, $qb$Read the change notice and ask if it applies immediately$qb$, $qb$Finish the task using the old process without checking$qb$, $qb$Restart the whole task using the new process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the change until your next task$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a quick task while you are working on something another manager marked urgent. You own the outcome, but multiple senior expectations compete for the same capacity. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and confirm the priority before changing focus$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the other urgent task and ask what they prefer$qb$, $qb$Do the newest request first$qb$, $qb$Continue the current task and ignore the new request$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
