  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new approved system now performs a calculation you used to do manually. The old operating approach still produces excellent local results but undermines consistency and handoffs across the organization. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Verify the new calculation during transition, then stop the manual duplicate once confidence is established$qb$, $qb$Compare the new result with your manual calculation for a short period$qb$, $qb$Keep doing both calculations permanently$qb$, $qb$Trust only the manual calculation for important work$qb$, $qb$Avoid the new system$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process changes while you are halfway through a task. The change spans teams with different operating realities while critical service commitments must continue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check which version applies to the current task and adjust accordingly$qb$, $qb$Read the change notice and ask if it applies immediately$qb$, $qb$Finish the task using the old process without checking$qb$, $qb$Restart the whole task using the new process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the change until your next task$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a quick task while you are working on something another manager marked urgent. You are accountable for the outcome while powerful stakeholders press incompatible priorities and expect a decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and confirm the priority before changing focus$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the other urgent task and ask what they prefer$qb$, $qb$Do the newest request first$qb$, $qb$Continue the current task and ignore the new request$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is absent and their small task blocks the rest of the group. Workstreams appear healthy individually, yet their choices are creating escalating program-level integration risk. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check the agreed backup or reassign the task clearly so work can continue$qb$, $qb$Ask who can cover the task and confirm the temporary owner$qb$, $qb$Let several people work on it until someone finishes$qb$, $qb$Wait for the teammate to return$qb$, $qb$Ignore the blocked work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring approval takes days even though most cases are low risk. The pilot succeeded, but enterprise scale will alter roles, controls, exception volumes, dependencies, and support needs. What is the best improvement approach?$qb$, $qb$Understand why approval is needed and test a simpler path for low-risk cases$qb$, $qb$Discuss whether low-risk cases need the same approval$qb$, $qb$Skip approval for an easy case to prove it is unnecessary$qb$, $qb$Remove the approval step for all cases$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because approvals reduce risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive unexpected criticism during a busy day and notice you are distracted afterward. The challenge is public and personal, but the decision that follows has material organizational consequences. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Pause, separate useful feedback from emotion, and return to the next priority$qb$, $qb$Take a short break and note any useful point from the feedback$qb$, $qb$Keep working while replaying the criticism in your mind$qb$, $qb$Send a defensive reply before you forget the details$qb$, $qb$Avoid the person who gave the feedback$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool suggests a number that does not match the source file. The AI result is persuasive, yet recent evidence and an organizational constraint absent from the model could reverse it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the source file to verify the correct number and investigate the mismatch$qb$, $qb$Check the source and correct the AI output$qb$, $qb$Average the two numbers$qb$, $qb$Use the AI number because it was calculated automatically$qb$, $qb$Ignore the mismatch$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new process removes a report you used to prepare every Friday. The old operating approach still produces excellent local results but undermines consistency and handoffs across the organization. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the new process covers the same need, then stop producing the old report$qb$, $qb$Follow the new process and monitor whether anyone still needs the report$qb$, $qb$Keep producing the report in case someone asks for it$qb$, $qb$Prepare it privately but do not send it$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new process and continue as before$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A familiar task is moved to a new system and your first attempt takes longer. The change spans teams with different operating realities while critical service commitments must continue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Continue learning the new system, use available guidance, and improve with practice$qb$, $qb$Review the guidance and try the task again$qb$, $qb$Return to the old system whenever the new one feels slow$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to use the new system for you$qb$, $qb$Avoid the task until the system becomes easier$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to 'make the report better' without saying what needs improvement. You are accountable for the outcome while powerful stakeholders press incompatible priorities and expect a decision. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what outcome, audience, or problem the improved report should address$qb$, $qb$Ask which part of the report they want changed$qb$, $qb$Add more detail because that usually improves reports$qb$, $qb$Copy the format of another report$qb$, $qb$Make changes based only on your own preference$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared task is delayed because one teammate did not know their part depended on another person's work. Workstreams appear healthy individually, yet their choices are creating escalating program-level integration risk. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Make dependencies and handoff points clear when the work is assigned$qb$, $qb$Add the dependencies to the shared task plan$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to communicate more often$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to monitor every handoff$qb$, $qb$Let each person manage dependencies themselves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring task requires the same information to be typed into two systems. The pilot succeeded, but enterprise scale will alter roles, controls, exception volumes, dependencies, and support needs. What is the best first improvement step?$qb$, $qb$Check why both entries are needed and whether one safe shared flow can remove duplication$qb$, $qb$Ask the system owners whether the duplicate entry can be reduced$qb$, $qb$Copy and paste between the systems faster$qb$, $qb$Stop entering the information in one system$qb$, $qb$Accept the duplication because both systems are official$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a rude email and want to reply immediately. The challenge is public and personal, but the next decision has material organizational consequences. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Pause, check the facts and purpose of your reply, then respond calmly$qb$, $qb$Draft a reply and review it after a short pause$qb$, $qb$Reply briefly so the issue is closed quickly$qb$, $qb$Forward the email to coworkers and ask what they think$qb$, $qb$Respond in the same tone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool creates a list of sources, but one link does not exist. The AI result is persuasive, yet recent evidence and an organizational constraint absent from the model could reverse it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify every important source before relying on the output$qb$, $qb$Check the links that support the main claims$qb$, $qb$Delete the broken link and trust the rest$qb$, $qb$Ask AI to replace the link without checking it$qb$, $qb$Use the list because only one link is wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new shared dashboard replaces a personal report you built and trust. The old operating approach still produces excellent local results but undermines consistency and handoffs across the organization. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Verify the dashboard covers the needed information, then stop duplicating the old report$qb$, $qb$Use both for a short period while checking consistency$qb$, $qb$Keep your personal report permanently$qb$, $qb$Use the dashboard only when someone asks$qb$, $qb$Reject the dashboard because your report is familiar$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new work rule is introduced with little notice. The change spans teams with different operating realities while critical service commitments must continue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the required change, apply it, and quickly clarify anything that could affect your work$qb$, $qb$Read the rule and ask focused questions where needed$qb$, $qb$Wait for teammates to show how they interpret it$qb$, $qb$Continue the old way until a manager checks$qb$, $qb$Ignore the rule because notice was short$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for an update and you have good progress but also one new risk. You own the outcome while powerful stakeholders press incompatible priorities and expect a decision. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Share the progress, explain the risk and impact, and state what decision or help is needed$qb$, $qb$Give the progress and flag the new risk clearly$qb$, $qb$Focus on the progress and mention the risk later$qb$, $qb$Report only the risk so it gets attention$qb$, $qb$Say everything is on track$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate completes their part, but another person does not know the handoff is ready. Workstreams look healthy individually, yet their decisions are creating escalating program-level integration risk. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Make the handoff visible to the next owner and confirm the next action$qb$, $qb$Notify the next person that the work is ready$qb$, $qb$Assume they will see the status in the system$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager the first part is complete$qb$, $qb$Wait until the next person asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team spends time copying the same status into three places. The pilot succeeded, but enterprise scale will alter roles, controls, exception volumes, dependencies, and support needs. What is the best first improvement?$qb$, $qb$Find which source is actually needed and test a way to update the information once$qb$, $qb$Map who uses each status copy and why$qb$, $qb$Copy the status faster using a template$qb$, $qb$Stop updating one location without checking$qb$, $qb$Accept the duplication because different teams use it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate's comment irritates you during a meeting, but the discussion must continue. The challenge is public and personal, but the next decision carries material organizational consequences. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Notice the reaction, stay with the issue, and respond to the facts rather than the irritation$qb$, $qb$Pause before replying and ask what the teammate means$qb$, $qb$Hide the irritation but keep arguing your point$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate their comment was irritating$qb$, $qb$Leave the meeting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI-generated answer includes a quotation that you cannot find in the source. The analysis may influence a material decision, yet recent evidence and a missing organizational constraint could reverse it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or correct the quotation after checking the original source$qb$, $qb$Search the source and verify the quotation before using it$qb$, $qb$Use the quotation without quotation marks$qb$, $qb$Ask AI to confirm the quotation$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the rest of the answer is accurate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new procedure removes a control you used for years because the control is now built into the system. The old approach still produces excellent local results but undermines consistency and handoffs across the organization. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Verify the system control works as intended, then stop duplicating the old manual control$qb$, $qb$Check the new control during transition and follow the updated procedure$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual control permanently$qb$, $qb$Use the old control only on important cases$qb$, $qb$Ignore the updated procedure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regulatory interpretation changes midway through implementation, invalidating part of the design but not the business objective. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Separate what is now non-compliant from what remains useful, redesign only what is necessary, and reset commitments using the new constraint.$qb$, $qb$Pause the entire program until regulators provide more detailed guidance.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current design while seeking an exemption because significant work is already complete.$qb$, $qb$Implement a temporary manual workaround and leave the underlying design unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Keep the original plan because changing late will create more delivery risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants a single recommendation today; your analysis shows two viable options with different risk profiles and no clearly dominant choice. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Recommend the option that best fits stated priorities, make the trade-offs explicit, and identify what evidence would trigger a change.$qb$, $qb$Present both options neutrally and ask your manager to choose between them.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lower-risk option because uncertainty makes caution preferable.$qb$, $qb$Keep analyzing until one option becomes clearly superior.$qb$, $qb$Select the option you think your manager already prefers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A matrix team keeps missing handoffs because each function plans capacity independently. More meetings have not helped. What change is most likely to improve performance?$qb$, $qb$Create shared end-to-end planning with visible dependencies, common milestones, and explicit cross-functional ownership for constraints.$qb$, $qb$Increase meeting frequency so emerging conflicts are discussed sooner.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to commit more contingency capacity to the work.$qb$, $qb$Create a central escalation list for missed handoffs.$qb$, $qb$Hold individuals accountable whenever their local task causes a downstream delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A competitor introduces a lower-cost digital service. Your current offer remains profitable, but customer switching has begun in one segment. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the profitable core while testing differentiated responses in the vulnerable segment and defining evidence-based triggers for broader change.$qb$, $qb$Match the competitor's price across all segments immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait until switching becomes material before investing in a response.$qb$, $qb$Launch a full replacement product because digital entrants usually scale quickly.$qb$, $qb$Increase marketing spend on the current offer without changing the proposition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In an executive meeting, a leader criticizes your team in a way you consider unfair. You notice your attention shifting from the issue to defending your reputation. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regain focus on the substance, ask for the specific evidence behind the concern, and respond after separating facts from the personal sting.$qb$, $qb$State that the criticism feels unfair, then explain your team's position.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the meeting ends and address the leader privately.$qb$, $qb$Defend the team's record immediately so silence is not mistaken for agreement.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the criticism because it was delivered emotionally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit wants to automate a high-volume decision using AI. The model performs well on average but poorly on a small, high-impact class of cases. What is the best deployment decision?$qb$, $qb$Design controls for the high-impact cases, set human review thresholds, monitor outcomes by segment, and scale only within those safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Deploy the model broadly because overall accuracy is strong and improve the weak class over time.$qb$, $qb$Use the model only as an advisory tool for all cases and let employees decide when to trust it.$qb$, $qb$Reject the technology until it performs equally well across every case type.$qb$, $qb$Deploy it only for the highest-impact cases because those create the greatest savings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A legacy process performs well, but new evidence shows one assumption behind it is no longer valid. What is the strongest response from an experienced leader?$qb$, $qb$Identify which decisions depend on the obsolete assumption, test alternatives, and retire only the practices that no longer hold.$qb$, $qb$Replace the whole process so the organization does not remain anchored to the past.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process until performance deteriorates visibly.$qb$, $qb$Add a new exception rule while leaving the underlying assumption untouched.$qb$, $qb$Ask newer employees to design a replacement because they are less attached to legacy practice.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new regulation and a disruptive competitor undermine your business model while a major transformation is already underway. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess the transformation, preserve still-valid investments, stop obsolete work, and redirect capacity through explicit choices.$qb$, $qb$Complete the current transformation before reacting.$qb$, $qb$Launch a second transformation program for the new threats.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to adapt independently within its budget.$qb$, $qb$Freeze major decisions until both threats are fully understood.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board sponsor insists on growth while the CEO has shifted emphasis toward cash generation. Both expect your portfolio plan next week. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Surface the conflict, translate each priority into portfolio consequences, recommend an integrated trade-off, and obtain alignment on decision criteria.$qb$, $qb$Follow the CEO's latest direction because it is operationally authoritative.$qb$, $qb$Keep the growth plan and add a cash-improvement workstream.$qb$, $qb$Prepare two separate plans and let each stakeholder choose.$qb$, $qb$Reduce every initiative's budget proportionally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Three acquired businesses must integrate, but each has a strong identity and different operating strengths. What is the best principle?$qb$, $qb$Standardize only enterprise outcomes and interfaces requiring commonality, preserve valuable local strengths, and create mechanisms for interdependent work.$qb$, $qb$Move all three onto the largest unit's operating model.$qb$, $qb$Keep each business independent for a year.$qb$, $qb$Rotate leaders among businesses so integration happens through relationships.$qb$, $qb$Combine teams immediately and let a new culture emerge.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A technology could reshape your industry's economics, but its adoption curve is highly uncertain. What is the strongest strategic posture?$qb$, $qb$Invest enough to learn and secure options, define signals for scaling or exiting, and avoid commitments dependent on one forecast.$qb$, $qb$Commit early at scale because waiting risks strategic position.$qb$, $qb$Wait until customer demand proves the technology is established.$qb$, $qb$Acquire a specialist company and let it manage the uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current strategy until annual planning.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a board meeting, a director questions your integrity after misunderstanding a data point. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regulate your reaction, correct the misunderstanding calmly, address the integrity implication directly, and offer evidence without personalizing the exchange.$qb$, $qb$Correct the data point and ignore the personal implication.$qb$, $qb$Ask the chair to stop the discussion.$qb$, $qb$Challenge the director's interpretation firmly.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and send a rebuttal afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your company is considering AI recommendations for workforce reductions using productivity, cost and performance data. What governance approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use AI as decision support with validated data, bias and error testing, suitable explainability, human accountability, appeal routes and monitoring.$qb$, $qb$Let the system rank employees but require executives to approve the final list.$qb$, $qb$Use it only where historical performance data is complete.$qb$, $qb$Delay until AI systems become consistently accurate.$qb$, $qb$Automate decisions because algorithms are less biased than managers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A management practice you introduced years ago became a company standard, but evidence now shows it slows decisions without improving quality. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reopen the assumption publicly, test alternatives, retire or modify the practice based on evidence, and make it safe for others to challenge established methods.$qb$, $qb$Quietly allow teams to bypass it where they see fit.$qb$, $qb$Keep it until a replacement proves superior across the whole company.$qb$, $qb$Ask a new leader to review it so you are not judging your own design.$qb$, $qb$Defend it because removing standards too readily creates instability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sudden trade restriction makes a key component unavailable in one market, but the rule may be temporary. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect immediate continuity, identify reversible substitutes and routing options, and set evidence-based triggers for deeper structural changes.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The chief executive asks for a cost reduction that conflicts with a board-approved growth commitment. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Translate the request into strategic consequences, identify choices that preserve intent, and obtain explicit alignment on remaining trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$After a merger, legacy teams attend common meetings but still route important work through old networks. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign shared workflows, decision rights and measures so important outcomes require cross-legacy collaboration.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A mature business faces a smaller entrant whose model makes money differently and serves customers your economics cannot profitably reach. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Understand the entrant's economic engine and customer trade-offs, then test responses that need not copy its visible product.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a tense investment meeting, criticism of your proposal triggers a strong urge to defend months of work. What action best protects the decision?$qb$, $qb$Notice the reaction, separate personal investment from evidence, and invite the strongest challenge to assumptions before responding.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI forecasting system materially outperforms planners overall but performs poorly during rare market shocks. How should an executive use it?$qb$, $qb$Use its strengths while defining conditions requiring human challenge, alternative scenarios and monitoring for regime changes.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership practice you introduced years ago is now slowing a larger organization, but managers continue it because they believe you still value it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explicitly withdraw the old expectation, explain what changed, model the new behavior, and make it safe to challenge other outdated practices.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A competitor changes customer expectations faster than your annual operating cycle can respond. What should an executive do?$qb$, $qb$Create a shorter learning-and-decision cadence, redirect bounded resources as evidence changes, and protect essential operations.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The CEO gives a verbal instruction that appears inconsistent with a recently approved executive-committee decision. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the intended change, show the conflict and consequences, and confirm the revised priority and governance path before redirecting work.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership team is polite in meetings but settles difficult issues through private alliances beforehand. What should the leader change?$qb$, $qb$Bring consequential trade-offs into shared forums, clarify decision rules, and address incentives that make private alignment more effective.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your successful product may become less relevant if an emerging customer behavior persists, but evidence is mixed. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Protect current value while testing the behavior, investing in options and defining signals that justify larger resource shifts.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$At a hostile regulatory hearing, a question wrongly implies your team concealed information. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Manage your reaction, correct the premise precisely, answer the legitimate issue, and avoid turning factual correction into personal confrontation.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool flags supplier risk accurately overall, but some high-risk ratings are hard to explain. What should governance require?$qb$, $qb$Match use to consequence: validate data and performance, require reviewable evidence for consequential flags, assign human accountability, and monitor errors.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A management ritual you introduced when the company was smaller now consumes substantial leadership time with little evidence of value. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Make its original assumptions explicit, test whether they still hold, retire or redesign it based on evidence, and model willingness to reverse your own practice.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sanctions change may make an important market inaccessible with little notice. Exiting early destroys value; waiting could strand assets. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Create staged, reversible exposure reductions and explicit legal and commercial triggers while protecting continuity and preparing an orderly exit path.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board chair and CEO give different signals about how aggressively to pursue an acquisition. What should you do before mobilizing the deal team?$qb$, $qb$Translate the difference into concrete risk, price and strategic boundaries and obtain explicit alignment through the proper governance route.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A newly formed executive team agrees in meetings but repeatedly reopens decisions afterward within functional groups. What should the leader change?$qb$, $qb$Clarify decision rules, record commitments and dissent, and address why leaders feel safer or more rewarded reopening decisions than executing them.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable service may be displaced by a platform model that changes where value is captured. What is the strongest strategic response?$qb$, $qb$Study the new value chain and economics, test alternative positions and partnerships, and build options before current profitability obscures structural change.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A director criticizes your team unfairly in front of the board, and you know a private email would disprove the claim. What is the strongest immediate response?$qb$, $qb$Control the impulse to retaliate, correct the material fact succinctly, keep confidential material protected, and arrange appropriate evidence-based follow-up.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI system can identify likely contract risks faster than lawyers but sometimes misses novel clauses. What is the strongest operating model?$qb$, $qb$Use it for triage with validated boundaries, require expert review where novelty or consequence is high, and monitor misses to update controls and models.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A reporting process you designed years ago is now widely bypassed because teams find it slow. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Revisit the original purpose and assumptions, learn from the workarounds, and redesign or retire the process rather than defending it because you created it.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new government suddenly changes industrial policy in a market where you have committed capital but not yet built the facility. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Revalidate the investment against new scenarios, preserve reversible options, engage relevant stakeholders, and stage further commitments against explicit policy signals.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a rapid organizational change that conflicts with commitments you have made to your own leadership team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the new priority and rationale, surface the commitments it displaces, recommend a transition, and align before asking your team to change direction.$qb$, $qb$In this case, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$At first, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-company initiative has strong relationships but poor execution because nobody knows who can make final decisions. What intervention matters most?$qb$, $qb$Define outcome ownership, decision rights and escalation boundaries across the participating groups while preserving collaborative input.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A disruptive technology could reduce demand for a highly profitable service but also enable a new revenue model your company is poorly equipped to run. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess both erosion and creation mechanisms, build capabilities through staged experiments or partnerships, and set triggers for shifting resources between models.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$At first, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board member dismisses a junior executive's valid concern in a way that visibly unsettles the room. You chair the meeting. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the discussion composed, bring the concern back to its evidence and relevance, ensure it is examined fairly, and address meeting behavior without escalating personally.$qb$, $qb$At first, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$In this case, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI model recommends which customer complaints deserve human investigation. False negatives are rare but could conceal serious safety issues. What design is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Set risk-based human review and override routes, validate rare-event performance, monitor misses, and keep accountable owners for safety-sensitive decisions.$qb$, $qb$In this case, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An organizational principle you once championed is now being applied rigidly in situations where it harms outcomes. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the principle's purpose and boundaries, acknowledge where application has become dogmatic, and model adapting the practice without abandoning the underlying intent.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sudden export-rule change threatens one product line, but lawyers expect clarification within weeks. What should leadership do now?$qb$, $qb$Protect continuity with reversible alternatives, identify decision triggers, and avoid irreversible restructuring until uncertainty materially resolves.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The CEO asks you to accelerate a launch, while the risk committee requires a control that makes the date unlikely. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Present the conflict, consequences and feasible choices to the decision owners, recommend a path, and obtain an explicit decision on residual risk and timing.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-functional executive group reaches consensus, then functions reinterpret decisions during execution. What should the group leader change?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which decisions require consultation versus consent, record outcomes and boundaries, and hold owners accountable for executing agreed choices.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and schedule the broader structural question for the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the resulting consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current course until the risk becomes certain enough to force action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable business depends on an intermediary that a new digital model could make unnecessary. Customers have not yet moved at scale. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Test how value shifts if intermediation declines, build direct-channel options, and define signals for reallocating investment before economics deteriorate.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A peer publicly questions your competence during a time-critical executive meeting. You have evidence their claim is wrong. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regulate your response, correct only the material factual point, refocus on the decision, and address the personal conduct separately if needed.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI system recommends credit limits faster than analysts, but performance varies significantly for new customer segments. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use segment-specific validation and thresholds, retain accountable human review where evidence is weak or impact high, and monitor outcomes before expanding autonomy.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership rule you championed years ago now discourages experimentation because managers apply it literally. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the original intent and changed context, explicitly release the obsolete interpretation, and model adapting the practice when evidence no longer supports it.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and schedule the broader structural question for the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the resulting consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current course until the risk becomes certain enough to force action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A political transition may alter rules affecting a large investment, but the direction is unclear and construction has already begun. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Separate irreversible from deferrable commitments, prepare scenarios and trigger points, engage appropriately, and preserve options while continuing no-regret work.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek more stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your supervisor wants a public commitment before your team has completed analysis of a material risk. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Explain what is known, what remains decision-critical, and the consequence of committing now; recommend the earliest responsible commitment point or an explicitly conditional commitment.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global program has clear executive sponsorship, but regional teams create local versions that undermine interoperability. What should the program leader change?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the few non-negotiable interfaces and decision rights, permit bounded local adaptation elsewhere, and govern deviations against shared outcomes.$qb$, $qb$Take a useful immediate step on the visible issue, but defer the wider structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
